u00d72, F3\u00d72, F4\u00d72  \n  - Singles: T1, B1, B2, B4, B8  \n\nI lack a clear direction. But note:\n- I can aim for **All Pungs** (6 points) if I can form more pungs.\n- I already have one pung (W1), and three pairs: possibly convert F3, F4 into pungs?\n- F3 is Seat Wind \u2192 valuable if made into pung (2 points).\n- F4 has no special value (not prevalent or seat wind).\n- Therefore, **keeping F3 is better than F4**.\n- But I have two of each \u2014 so discarding one F4 keeps a pair intact, which is fine.\n\nAlso, consider safety:\n- J2 has been discarded multiple times (by me and others), now zero left \u2014 very safe to assume it's dead.\n- B5 is fully exposed (konged) \u2014 cannot be drawn or used.\n- T6, T8, etc., have been played.\n\nBest move should balance safety and potential.\n\nPromising options:\n- **Play B4**: Not part of any strong sequence now (B5 gone), so hard to complete B4-B5-B6. Safe.\n- **Play T1**: Could complete T1-T2-T3, but T2 and T3 haven\u2019t been seen much. Next player discarded T1 earlier \u2014 maybe not building T-chows.\n- **Play B8**: B8 could go into 6-7-8 or 7-8-9. B7 was discarded once, B6 twice \u2014 possible, but not highly active.\n- **Play F4**: Slightly safer than F3 because F3 is Seat Wind. But both are winds \u2014 generally safer than random suited tiles late game.\n- However, **if I keep both F3 and F4**, I preserve flexibility for future pungs.\n\nBut I must discard something.\n\nKey insight:\n- I already have **Dragon Pung** (J1\u00d73 by opponent doesn\u2019t count for me), but I don\u2019t have dragon melds.\n- I have **no chows**, so **All Chows** is impossible.\n- Best chance: **All Pungs** (6 points) + possibly **Seat Wind** (F3 pung, 2 points) + **Concealed Hand** if I draw all myself.\n- Currently, I\u2019ve done a Peng (W1), so **Fully Concealed Hand** is lost, but **Concealed Hand** (if I win off a discard) is still possible as long as all other melds are self-drawn \u2014 but I already Peng\u2019d, so even that is invalid? Wait:\n\nWait: \u201cConcealed Hand\u201d pattern: *A hand with no tiles from other players except the winning tile* \u2014 since I used Peng (took W1 from discard), I have already incorporated a tile from another player. So **Concealed Hand is unattainable**.\n\nSimilarly, **Melded Hand** requires all melds to be formed from other players\u2019 discards \u2014 mine isn\u2019t, since I drew most tiles.\n\nSo better to focus on **All Pungs (6 points)** and try to collect pungs.\n\nI have:\n- One pung: W1\n- Three pairs: W3, F3, F4\n\nIf I can turn any of those into pungs, I get closer to All Pungs (needs four pungs/kongs).\n\nThus, keeping pairs is good.\n\nBut I need to discard safely.\n\nNow compare danger:\n- **B1, B2, B4, B8**: All suited tiles that could complete chows.\n   - B1+B2 \u2192 needs B3 for 1-2-3\n   - B2+B4 \u2192 not sequential\n   - B4 \u2192 could be middle for 3-4-5, but B3/B5? B5 is gone, so 3-4-5 impossible.\n   - B8 \u2192 could be in 6-7-8 or 7-8-9; B7 was discarded, B6 twice \u2014 possible, but not high frequency.\n- **T1**: Could be in 1-2-3; T2 and T3 exist, but not heavily collected.\n- **W3**: Could be in 1-2-3 or 2-3-4? But W1 and W2 are already punged by others \u2014 W1 by me, W2 by next player. So W3 is unlikely to help anyone build chow \u2014 because they would need W1-W2-W3, but W1 and W2 are mostly taken. Hence, **W3 is very safe to discard**.\n\nYes! Critical observation:\n- There are only 4 copies of each tile.\n- W1: 3 used in my pung \u2192 only 1 left unshown.\n- W2: 3 used in next player\u2019s pung \u2192 only 1 left unshown.\n- So only one W3 available, and it's unlikely someone holds W1+W2 to make chow with W3.\n- Similarly, W3-W4-W5: possible, but W3 is rare now \u2014 so discarding W3 is **very safe**.\n\nAdditionally, I have two W3s \u2014 I don\u2019t need both. Discarding one doesn\u2019t hurt my hand \u2014 I keep a pair.\n\nCompare to F3/F4: both are wind pairs \u2014 potentially valuable for pungs.\n\nSo among all choices, **Play W3** is safe and non-committal.\n\nOther considerations:\n- Could I be heading toward **Seven Pairs**? I have several pairs: W3, F3, F4 \u2014 and could draw more. But I have too many singles. Unlikely.\n- **Full Flush**? No \u2014 mixed suits.\n- **Half Flush**? Mostly not \u2014 I have W1 pung, W3s, but also T1, Bs, Fs \u2014 too diverse.\n\nNo clear high-scoring hand. Best to play safe and preserve flexibility.\n\n**Best safe discard: W3**\n\nReason: W3 is extremely safe due to W1 and W2 being heavily used in pungs by myself and the next player, making it nearly impossible for others to use W3 in a chow. Discarding one W3 preserves a pair while minimizing risk. Other tiles like B1, B2, B4, B8, T1 are more likely to complete chows in their respective suits.\n\nAnswer: Play W3"}
