ust have drawn it back \u2014 possible.\n\nSo I can discard it again.\n\nIt\u2019s acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, **B4** \u2014 I also played B4 earlier (\"Play B4\"), so I discarded it, but now I have it \u2014 so I drew it again.\n\nSame situation.\n\nBut between B4 and F4, **F4 is less useful** because:\n- B4 is a suited tile, could be part of chow (e.g., 345, 234, 456)\n- F4 is irrelevant wind\n\nSo **F4 is worse to keep**.\n\nAlso, discarding F4 avoids helping others with possible wind pungs.\n\nBut since F4 unshown:3, and only one in my hand, others might not be collecting.\n\nStill, better to keep suited tiles for flexibility.\n\nThus, **Play F4** is the best choice.\n\nEvaluation: Among the valid actions, discarding F4 removes an irrelevant honor tile (North Wind) that does not contribute to my Seat Wind (West) or Prevailing Wind (East). I already have a pair of F3 (West Wind), which is valuable for the Seat Wind pattern (2 points), so keeping F3 is important. F4 is redundant and offers no strategic value. Other discards like B4 or W8 have slightly more potential for future chows, so F4 is the safest and most logical discard.\nReason: F4 is a non-scoring, non-matching wind tile that hinders hand development; removing it improves efficiency without risk.\nAnswer: Play F4"}
