d concealed hand tiles.\n\nTherefore:\n- For W3: you have 1, previous player has 3 in meld \u2192 total 4 \u2192 so no more exist \u2192 unshown should be 0, but listed as 1 \u2192 impossible.\n\nContradiction indicates possible data issue, but we must proceed.\n\nAssume the provided \"unshown\" counts are accurate, meaning not all tiles are yet accounted for visibly.\n\nBut logically: if previous player Peng\u2019d W3, they have 3; you have 1 in hand \u2192 total 4 \u2192 no more exist \u2192 unshown must be 0.\n\nYet listed as 1 \u2192 error.\n\nLikely explanation: your hand does NOT have W3 anymore? Let's recheck:\n\nYour hand: [W3:1; ...] \u2014 yes, says you have one W3.\n\nBut history: Play W3 \u2014 you played W3 earlier? When?\n\nHistory: Play B4, Play J2, Play T6, Play B8, Play J3, Peng W1, Play W9, Play B5, Play W3, ...\n\nAh! You **played W3** \u2014 meaning you discarded it!\n\nBut then how do you have W3:1 in hand now?\n\nThat\u2019s a conflict.\n\nUnless... did you draw another W3 after discarding one?\n\nBut there are only four W3 total.\n\nTrace:\n\nInitially: 4 W3 tiles.\n\nWho has them?\n\n- You drew one W3 at some point, then **discarded it** (Play W3 in history)\n- Previous player then Peng\u2019d W3 \u2192 they collected a discard (likely yours) to complete their pung\n\u2192 So they now have 3 W3\n\u2192 You previously held one and discarded it\n\u2192 So you should not have W3 now\n\nBut your current hand says W3:1 \u2192 contradiction\n\nThus, either:\n- You later drew back a W3 (impossible, since all 4 are used: 3 with opponent, 1 discarded by you and taken), or\n- Data inconsistency\n\nMost plausible: **you do not actually have W3 in hand** \u2014 it's a display error.\n\nBut the system says \"Your hand tiles are: [W3:1; ...]\" \u2014 so we must assume you have it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"Play W3\" was a mistake, or refers to different context.\n\nWait \u2014 perhaps \"Play W3\" means you discarded a different W3? But only four exist.\n\nImpossible for both you to have discarded one and still hold one unless you drew it back \u2014 but previous player Peng\u2019d it from someone \u2014 likely you.\n\nGiven the confusion, let's cross-check W3 availability:\n\nUnshown: W3:1 \u2192 so one remains somewhere (wall or hidden)\n\nBut previous player has [W3,W3,W3] \u2192 3\n\nYou have W3:1 \u2192 1\n\nTotal 4 \u2192 unshown should be 0\n\nBut listed as 1 \u2192 therefore, **the unshown count is wrong**\n\nSimilarly, J2: unshown:0, and no one has it in meld except previous player played it thrice, you played it, etc. \u2014 consistent.\n\nSo trust the hand and meld states more than unshown list.\n\nPossibility: unshown list includes only wall tiles, not concealed hand tiles? But that would make it inaccurate for decision-making.\n\nBetter to rely on observed plays.\n\nFrom history:\n- You played W3 \u2192 you discarded it\n- Then previous player Peng\u2019d W3 \u2192 they claimed it \u2192 so you cannot have W3 now\n\nTherefore, **your hand should not contain W3**\n\nLikely typo in state description.\n\nSimilarly, you played W9 twice? Play W9 appears twice in history? No:\n\nHistory: Play W9 (once), then later Play W3, etc.\n\nYou have W9:1 in hand \u2014 possible if you drew it back.\n\nBut W9 unshown:2, total 4, you have 1, no one else has W9 in meld \u2192 possible.\n\nBack to logic: **you cannot have W3 because you discarded it and it was used to complete an opponent\u2019s Peng**.\n\nTherefore, despite being listed, **W3 is not in your hand**.\n\nSame for W1: you Peng\u2019d W1 \u2192 used 3 tiles, not in hand now.\n\nHand list: W3:1 \u2014 probably error.\n\nLook at valid actions: [Play B4; Play W3; Play T9; Play W9; Play W8; Play T8; Play B9; Play F3; Play B8; Play B1]\n\nIf you don\u2019t have W3, you cannot play it.\n\nBut it's in the valid actions list \u2014 suggests maybe you do have it?\n\nUnless the game allows invalid suggestions.\n\nTo resolve: assume the **valid actions** determine what you can do.\n\nSince \"Play W3\" is listed as valid, you must have W3.\n\nBut how?\n\nUnless you drew a W3 after discarding it? Impossible.\n\nOnly possibility: the \"Play W3\" in history was not W3 tile \u2014 but misrecorded?\n\nNo \u2014 \"Play W3\" clearly means discard W3.\n\nFinal conclusion: accept the state as given, even if inconsistent.\n\nProceed conservatively.\n\nAssume you have W3:1 \u2014 somehow.\n\nBut risk of playing W3: previous player already has pung of W3, so they won\u2019t need another \u2014 cannot rob your discard to win (unless making Kong, but rare). Still, safer not to give risky tiles.\n\nBetter to discard less dangerous tiles.\n\nNow evaluate promising moves:\n\nKeep:\n- F3 pair: for Seat Wind (2 pts), possible Peng later\n- B1, B9, T9, W9: terminals, could support Outside Hand\n- But no strong coordination\n\nDiscard candidates:\n- B4: number 4, simple tile, not terminal, not useful for Outside Hand\n- T8: 8 is terminal, part of upper range, but you have T8 and T9 \u2014 could form T7-8-9 chow if you get T7\n- W8, W9: same, W7-8-9 possible\n- B1: terminal, but alone\n- B8, B9: B8+B9, need B7 for chow\n- But B7 unshown:3, possible\n\nHowever, chi is only from previous player, who hasn\u2019t shown much bamboo.\n\nMore importantly, focus on safety and hand direction.\n\nYou already have a pung of W1 (terminal honor), and F3 pair (honors).\n\nIf you can form sets using terminals/honors, aim for **Outside Hand (4 pts)**.\n\nAlso, F3 pung would give **Seat Wind (2 pts)** and **Dragon/Honor pung (1 pt)**.\n\nOther tiles like B4 is a \"simple\" (middle tile), which breaks Outside Hand.\n\nTherefore, **B4 is the worst tile for your current strategy** \u2014 it doesn't fit Outside Hand, not terminal, not honor.\n\nWhereas B1, B8, B9, T8, T9, W8, W9 are all terminals \u2014 keeping them supports Outside Hand possibility.\n\nF3 pair is valuable \u2014 keep.\n\nSo best discard is **B4**, as it is the only non-terminal, non-honor suited tile in hand.\n\nIt contributes nothing to Outside Hand, whereas every other tile does.\n\nEven if you never complete chows, discarding B4 minimizes damage to scoring potential.\n\nAlso, B4 unshown:3 \u2014 plenty left, not safe or dangerous particularly.\n\nConclusion: **Play B4**\n\nEvaluation: Among valid discards, B4 is a simple tile (number 4) that does not contribute to Outside Hand or any terminal-based pattern, while all other tiles (B1, B8, B9, T8, T9, W8, W9, F3) are terminals or honors and support the Outside Hand pattern (4 points). Keeping F3 pair also enables Seat Wind (2 points) if promoted to pung. Thus, B4 is the weakest tile.\nReason: Discarding B4 maximizes potential for scoring patterns like Outside Hand by removing a non-terminal, non-honor tile, while retaining flexibility with terminals and honors.\nAnswer: Play B4"}
