t's check:\n\nSuited tiles in hand: W9, T7, T8, T9, B8, B8, B9 \u2192 all are 7,8,9. Even melded W1\u00d74 \u2014 wait, W1 is number 1 \u2192 terminal, not middle/upper. So **W1 kong disqualifies Upper Tiles**, because **Upper Tiles** requires *only* 7,8,9 suited tiles. Similarly, W1 is 1 \u2192 invalid.\n\nSo **cannot get Upper Tiles** due to W1 kong.\n\nSimilarly, **Full Flush**: all same suit? No \u2014 multiple suits present.\n\nBest possible patterns:\n- **All Pungs**: you already have a kong (W1), can add B8 pung \u2192 then need two more pungs. Remaining: T7,T8,T9,B9,F2,F3\u00d72 \u2014 hard to make pungs here.\n- **Outside Hand**: every meld contains terminal or honor. Your W1 kong qualifies (terminal). B8 pung? B8 is *not* terminal \u2192 invalidates Outside Hand. So forming B8 pung kills Outside Hand possibility.\n- Hence, **do not want to form B8 pung** if pursuing Outside Hand.\n\nWait \u2014 B8 is not terminal \u2192 any meld with B8 (unless part of [B7,B8,B9]) would break Outside Hand.\n\nBut if you keep B8 for chow [B7,B8,B9], then it\u2019s a chow involving non-terminal \u2192 still breaks Outside Hand.\n\nOnly way Outside Hand works is if all melds are:\n- Pungs/kongs of terminals (1 or 9) or honors\n- Chows that include a terminal, i.e., [1,2,3] or [7,8,9]\n\n[B7,B8,B9] is acceptable for Outside Hand (contains terminal B9).\n\nBut [B8,B8,B8] is three B8s \u2014 not terminal \u2192 invalid for Outside Hand.\n\nTherefore:\n- Forming **Peng B8** creates a non-terminal pung \u2192 disqualifies **Outside Hand**\n- Keeping B8 for possible [B7,B8,B9] chow allows Outside Hand eligibility\n\nMoreover, you have:\n- T7,T8,T9 \u2192 chow (non-terminal, but includes T7/T9? T9 is terminal \u2192 yes, T9 is 9 \u2192 so [T7,T8,T9] qualifies as it contains terminal T9\n- Similarly, [B7,B8,B9] contains B9 \u2192 qualifies\n- W9 is single, but could pair or go into [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 but W7 only one left, W8 one left \u2014 unlikely\n\nSo possible path:\n- Aim for **Outside Hand** (8 points): requires all melds and pair to contain at least one terminal or honor\n- Currently: W1 kong \u2192 valid (W1 is terminal)\n- Potential:\n  - [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 contains T9 \u2192 valid\n  - [B7,B8,B9] \u2192 contains B9 \u2192 valid\n  - Pair: F3 (honor) \u2192 valid\n  - Then need one more meld: you have F2, F3 \u2014 maybe [F2,F2,F2]? Only one F2 in hand, one possibly available\n  - Or [F3,F3,F3] \u2014 you have two F3, one possibly out\n\nBut F3: total 4 copies, unshown: F3:4 \u2192 all four still unshown? Wait, no: players may have them.\n\nWait, unshown count: F3:4 \u2192 means 4 remain unseen \u2192 possible.\n\nBut current hand only has two F3 \u2014 need third.\n\nAlternatively, use T9\u00d72? Only one T9.\n\nAnother idea: **All Honors and Terminals?** No \u2014 B8 is not terminal.\n\nNo \u2014 B8 is 8 \u2192 not terminal.\n\nThus, **any use of B8 in a meld that doesn't involve B9 as terminal makes it ineligible for Outside Hand only if the meld lacks terminal/honor**.\n\nBut [B8,B8,B8] has no terminal \u2192 invalid.\n\nWhereas [B7,B8,B9] has B9 \u2192 valid.\n\nHence, preserving B8 for chow is better.\n\nAdditionally, you might consider **Three-Suited Terminal Chows** or **Mixed Straight**, but those require specific structures.\n\nBut most importantly: **Peng B8** locks you into a non-terminal pung, reducing flexibility and killing Outside Hand potential.\n\nFurthermore, you have **Self-Drawn** potential if you draw final tile.\n\nAlso, **Concealed Hand** is gone anyway due to BuGang (exposed action).\n\nBut **Fully Concealed Hand** is lost.\n\nNow, is there any benefit to Peng B8?\n- Maybe speed up completion?\n- But you don\u2019t have third B8 \u2014 you have two, someone discards one \u2192 you can Peng.\n\nBut after Peng, your hand becomes:\n- Exposed: [B8,B8,B8]\n- Hand: W9, T7, T8, T9, B9, F2, F3, F3\n- Need two more melds and a pair.\n\nPossible:\n- [T7,T8,T9] chow \u2192 good\n- Then left: W9, B9, F2, F3\u00d72\n- Can try for [F3,F3,F3] or pair F3\n- But W9 and B9 are singles \u2014 no pair\n\nUnlikely to win quickly.\n\nIf you pass and keep B8, you preserve option for [B7,B8,B9] chow if you draw B7 or B9.\n\nB9: two unshown, possible.\n\nB7: three unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nAlternatively, draw F3 to complete dragon pung?\n\nBut J3 is White Dragon \u2014 you have no J-tiles.\n\nF3 is West Wind.\n\nYou have Seat Wind F3 \u00d72 \u2014 if you get a third, you can score **Seat Wind** (2 points) for having a pung of seat wind (West).\n\nCurrently you have pair of F3 \u2014 if you get third, can make pung.\n\nThat\u2019s promising.\n\nSo better strategy:\n- Keep F3 pair for potential pung \u2192 scores **Seat Wind** (2 pts) + **Dragon/Wind Pung** equivalent (but Wind, so counts under Prevalent/Seat Wind)\n- Also, Prevailing Wind is East (F1), you have no F1 \u2192 no Prevalent Wind bonus\n- But Seat Wind is West (F3) \u2192 if you make F3 pung, you get 2 pts\n\nSo aim:\n- Make [T7,T8,T9] chow\n- Make [B7,B8,B9] chow (if possible)\n- Make F3 pung or at least pair\n- Use W9 as single tile \u2014 but need pair\n\nWait \u2014 you have only one W9.\n\nUnless you draw another W9 (two left), but W9 is in high demand.\n\nAlternatively, discard W9 to reduce reliance.\n\nBut currently, you must choose between Peng B8 or Pass.\n\nGiven:\n- Peng B8 harms strategic flexibility\n- Enables a pung of non-terminal B8, which scores nothing directly and blocks Outside Hand\n- No significant point gain from B8 pung\n- Better to wait for possible chow or safer discard\n\nAnd critically: **is Peng B8 even possible?**\n\nThe valid actions list says [Peng B8; Pass], implying B8 was just discarded.\n\nBut previous player\u2019s history ends with \u201cPlay T2\u201d, not B8.\n\nNext player played T4, etc.\n\nNo B8 discard seen.\n\nPossibility: typo or hidden move.\n\nBut per instruction, we must accept the valid actions as given.\n\nSo assuming B8 *was* discarded, you can Peng.\n\nBut doing so is disadvantageous.\n\nBest decision: **Pass**\n\nPreserve hand flexibility, avoid committing to a weak pung, maintain chance at Outside Hand via chows ending in 9s, and pursue Seat Wind F3 pung.\n\nReason: Forming a pung of B8 (a middle tile) reduces the chance of achieving higher-scoring patterns like Outside Hand and offers minimal direct benefit. Passing preserves flexibility to complete chows with terminals (e.g., [B7,B8,B9]) and pursue valuable pairs or pungs in winds/dragons.\n\nAnswer: Pass"}
